  <!-- Table of Contents -->
  <div class="toc">
    <h3>Contents</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#overview">Bestseller Market Overview (2009–2019)</a></li>
      <li><a href="#correlation">Price vs. User Rating: Correlation Analysis</a></li>
      <li><a href="#genre">Fiction vs. Non-Fiction: Pricing &amp; Rating Strategies</a></li>
      <li><a href="#temporal">Year-over-Year Trends in Pricing and Ratings</a></li>
      <li><a href="#authors">Dominant Authors on the Bestseller List</a></li>
      <li><a href="#recommendations">Strategic Recommendations</a></li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- Executive Summary -->
  <div class="exec-summary">
    <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
    <ul class="exec-bullets">
      <li><strong>Price does not drive satisfaction.</strong> The Pearson correlation between price and user rating is <strong>r = −0.13</strong> — effectively zero. Higher-priced books show no meaningful advantage in customer ratings.</li>
      <li><strong>Budget books ($0–$9) are the sweet spot.</strong> They earn the highest average rating (4.68/5.0) and account for 43% of all bestseller entries — dominated by Fiction (54%).</li>
      <li><strong>Non Fiction commands premium prices but lower engagement.</strong> Avg. price $14.84 vs. Fiction's $10.85, yet generates only 9,065 avg. reviews vs. Fiction's 15,684.</li>
      <li><strong>Ratings climbed while prices fell.</strong> Average price dropped from $15.40 (2009) to $10.08 (2019) — a 35% decline — while average ratings rose from 4.58 to 4.74 and review volumes tripled.</li>
      <li><strong>2015 was the pivotal year.</strong> Prices hit a decade low ($10.42), review counts surged, and ratings began their sustained upward climb — marking a structural shift in the market.</li>
      <li><strong>Children's book authors dominate for longevity.</strong> Jeff Kinney, Dr. Seuss, Dav Pilkey, and Eric Carle command top frequency rankings with ratings above 4.8 and budget-tier prices.</li>
